    "question": "Given an array of integers nums and an integer target, return indices of the two numbers that add up to target. Exactly one solution exists; you may not use the same element twice.",
    "example": "Example 1:\nInput: nums = [2,7,11,15], target = 9\nOutput: [0,1]\nBecause nums[0] + nums[1] == 9\n\nExample 2:\nInput: nums = [3,2,4], target = 6\nOutput: [1,2]",
    "thinking": "Do not sort if you need original indices. Brute force is two nested loops O(n²). Upgrade: one pass with a hash map. For each x, ask “have I seen target − x?” If yes, return those indices.",
    "explain": "## Intuition\nImagine a checkout: you hold item 2 and need a 7 to hit 9. Instead of scanning the whole cart every time, keep a sticky note of what you already saw. When 7 appears, the note already has 2 at index 0 — done. That sticky note is a hash map.\n\n## Step-by-step (count / map approach)\n1. Create an empty map value → index.\n2. For each index i with value x:\n   - need = target − x\n   - If need is in the map, return [map[need], i]\n   - Else store x → i\n3. Guaranteed one answer, so we always find it.\n\n## Why O(n)\nEach element is processed once; hash map get/put is average O(1).",
    "naiveApproach": "For every i, scan j > i and test nums[i]+nums[j]==target. Correct but O(n²).",
    "optimizedApproach": "One-pass hash map of complements. Space O(n) for time O(n).",
    "patternFlow": "nums: 2 7 11 15   target=9\ni=0 x=2 need=7 map={} → store 2→0\ni=1 x=7 need=2 map has 2 → return [0,1]",
    "important": "Return indices, not values. Same index not allowed. Do not sort if you need indices.",
    "snippetJava": "int need = target - nums[i];\nif (map.containsKey(need)) return new int[]{map.get(need), i};\nmap.put(nums[i], i);",
    "snippetPython": "need = target - x\nif need in seen: return [seen[need], i]\nseen[x] = i",
    "java": "class Solution {\n    public int[] twoSum(int[] nums, int target) {\n        Map<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n            int need = target - nums[i];\n            if (map.containsKey(need)) {\n                return new int[] { map.get(need), i };\n            }\n            map.put(nums[i], i);\n        }\n        return new int[] {};\n    }\n}",
    "python": "class Solution:\n    def twoSum(self, nums: List[int], target: int) -> List[int]:\n        seen = {}\n        for i, x in enumerate(nums):\n            need = target - x\n            if need in seen:\n                return [seen[need], i]\n            seen[x] = i\n        return []",
