    "explain": "## Goal\nSolve Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array using the Two Pointers pattern so a junior engineer can follow every step.\n\n## Intuition\nTwo pointers turn nested loops into one smart pass. Place lo and hi, then move the pointer that can still improve the answer. After sorting (when allowed), most pair problems become O(n) or O(n²) for triplets instead of O(n³).\n\n## How to think in the interview\n1. Hear the signal: Sorted array, pair/triplet sum, palindrome, or in-place remove.\n2. Write the template on the board:\n```\nlo, hi = 0, n - 1\nwhile lo < hi:\n  if good(lo, hi): record; move both or one\n  elif too_small: lo += 1\n  else: hi -= 1\n```\n3. Map the problem fields onto the template (what is the window? what is the heap key? what is the DP state?).\n4. Dry-run one example aloud — move pointers / update state / say the invariant each step.\n5. Code only after the dry-run succeeds.\n6. List edge cases before claiming you are done.\n\n## Why this beats random LeetCode grinding\nOnce you own Two Pointers, Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array is one instance of a family — not a unique puzzle. That is how you reach 80–90% interview coverage.",
