    "explain": "## Goal\nSolve Implement Queue using Stacks using the Queue & Monotonic Queue pattern so a junior engineer can follow every step.\n\n## Intuition\nQueues process in arrival order — perfect for BFS. A monotonic deque keeps candidates for window maximum in O(1) amortized get-max. Design problems (circular queue, queue via stacks) test API clarity.\n\n## How to think in the interview\n1. Hear the signal: BFS layers, sliding window max, recent calls.\n2. Write the template on the board:\n```\nq = deque([start])\nwhile q:\n  u = q.popleft()\n  for v in neighbors(u):\n    if not seen: seen; q.append(v)\n```\n3. Map the problem fields onto the template (what is the window? what is the heap key? what is the DP state?).\n4. Dry-run one example aloud — move pointers / update state / say the invariant each step.\n5. Code only after the dry-run succeeds.\n6. List edge cases before claiming you are done.\n\n## Why this beats random LeetCode grinding\nOnce you own Queue & Monotonic Queue, Implement Queue using Stacks is one instance of a family — not a unique puzzle. That is how you reach 80–90% interview coverage.",
