    "id": 151,
    "lc": 200,
    "slug": "number-of-islands",
    "title": "Number of Islands",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "patternSlug": "graph-traversal",
    "tags": [
      "Graph DFS / BFS",
      "Medium"
    ],
    "question": "Given an m x n 2D binary grid which represents a map of '1's (land) and '0's (water), return the number of islands. An island is surrounded by water and formed by connecting adjacent lands horizontally or vertically.",
    "example": "Input:\n[[\"1\",\"1\",\"0\"],\n [\"1\",\"1\",\"0\"],\n [\"0\",\"0\",\"1\"]]\nOutput: 2",
    "thinking": "Each unvisited land cell starts a new island. Flood-fill (DFS/BFS) marks the whole connected component so we do not count it again.",
    "explain": "## Intuition\nScan the grid. Every time you see a '1', that is a new island — explore all reachable land and sink it to '0' (or mark visited).\n\n## DFS flood fill\nFrom (r,c), mark visited, recurse to 4 neighbors that are land in bounds.\n\n## Complexity\nEach cell visited once → O(m·n).",
    "naiveApproach": "No sharing of visited state would recount — must mark.",
    "optimizedApproach": "DFS/BFS flood fill or Union-Find.",
    "patternFlow": "for each cell:\n  if land and not seen:\n    islands++\n    dfs/bfs mark component",
    "important": "4-direction only (unless problem says 8). Mark before recursing to avoid cycles.",
    "snippetJava": "if (r<0||c<0||r>=m||c>=n||grid[r][c]!='1') return;\ngrid[r][c]='0';\ndfs(r+1,c); dfs(r-1,c); dfs(r,c+1); dfs(r,c-1);",
    "snippetPython": "if r<0 or c<0 or r>=m or c>=n or grid[r][c]!='1': return\ngrid[r][c]='0'\nfor dr,dc in ((1,0),(-1,0),(0,1),(0,-1)): dfs(r+dr,c+dc)",
    "java": "class Solution {\n    public int numIslands(char[][] grid) {\n        int m = grid.length, n = grid[0].length, count = 0;\n        for (int r = 0; r < m; r++) {\n            for (int c = 0; c < n; c++) {\n                if (grid[r][c] == '1') {\n                    count++;\n                    dfs(grid, r, c);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return count;\n    }\n    private void dfs(char[][] g, int r, int c) {\n        if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= g.length || c >= g[0].length || g[r][c] != '1') return;\n        g[r][c] = '0';\n        dfs(g, r + 1, c); dfs(g, r - 1, c); dfs(g, r, c + 1); dfs(g, r, c - 1);\n    }\n}",
    "python": "class Solution:\n    def numIslands(self, grid: List[List[str]]) -> int:\n        if not grid: return 0\n        m, n = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n        def dfs(r, c):\n            if r<0 or c<0 or r>=m or c>=n or grid[r][c] != '1':\n                return\n            grid[r][c] = '0'\n            for dr, dc in ((1,0),(-1,0),(0,1),(0,-1)):\n                dfs(r+dr, c+dc)\n        count = 0\n        for r in range(m):\n            for c in range(n):\n                if grid[r][c] == '1':\n                    count += 1\n                    dfs(r, c)\n        return count",
    "complexity": "Time O(m·n), Space O(m·n) worst-case recursion",
    "followUp": "Max area of island; number of distinct islands by shape.",
    "tip": "Name the graph: cells are nodes; 4-dir edges.",
    "edgeCases": [
      "All water",
      "All land",
      "Single cell",
      "Diagonal-only touch (not connected)"
    ]
