    "explain": "## Goal\nSolve Subsets using the Backtracking pattern so a junior engineer can follow every step.\n\n## Intuition\nBacktracking builds candidates incrementally and abandons a path as soon as it cannot lead to a solution. Template: choose → recurse → undo. Deduplicate with sorting + skip-same-at-depth.\n\n## How to think in the interview\n1. Hear the signal: All subsets, permutations, combinations, boards.\n2. Write the template on the board:\n```\ndef bt(path, start):\n  record(path)\n  for i in start..n-1:\n    choose nums[i]; bt(path, i+1); undo\n```\n3. Map the problem fields onto the template (what is the window? what is the heap key? what is the DP state?).\n4. Dry-run one example aloud — move pointers / update state / say the invariant each step.\n5. Code only after the dry-run succeeds.\n6. List edge cases before claiming you are done.\n\n## Why this beats random LeetCode grinding\nOnce you own Backtracking, Subsets is one instance of a family — not a unique puzzle. That is how you reach 80–90% interview coverage.",
